<template>
  <DocHeader>
    Economic Drivers
  </DocHeader>
  <DocContent v-if="isLoaded">
    <aside
      class="mb-8 rounded-lg border border-amber-300 bg-amber-50 px-5 py-4 text-amber-950"
    >
      <strong class="block mb-1">Academic illustration only</strong>
      <p class="m-0">
        This page explains the economic levers and flow of the Argon system for academic purposes only. It does not
        predict or imply that any actual dollar returns can be generated. For the purposes of this page, all amounts
        are denominated in Argons, not dollars.
      </p>
    </aside>

    <p>
      Argon's economic model is an outlier in the crypto industry. It's financial upside is not dependent on its
      tokens increasing in value. Instead, the primary opportunity is in the underlying operational activities of the network.
    </p>

    <h2>Token Triumvirate</h2>
    <p>The most basic economic unit of the Argon Network is its three core tokens:</p>
    <div class="flex flex-row gap-4 text-center font-bold mt-10 mb-10">
      <div class="w-1/3 flex flex-col items-center">
        <ArgonIcon class="h-16 mb-3" />
        <div>Argon Stablecoin</div>
        <div class="font-light">("Argon")</div>
      </div>
      <div class="w-1/3 flex flex-col items-center">
        <ArgonotIcon class="h-16 mb-3" />
        <div>Argon Ownership Token</div>
        <div class="font-light">("Argonot")</div>
      </div>
      <div class="w-1/3 flex flex-col items-center">
        <BitcoinIcon class="h-16 mb-3" />
        <div>Bitcoin Token</div>
        <div class="font-light">("Bitcoin")</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      Each of these tokens plays a unique role in the ecosystem.
    </p>

    <h3>Argons Are the Network's Stable Currency</h3>
    <p>
      Although the Argon is a critical component needed to participate in network operations, the Argon
      stablecoin itself should never increase (or decrease) in value. It is designed to hold a steady purchasing power
      across time, with no inflation or deflation. For example, the same amount of Argons will buy you a comparable
      quantity of groceries, housing and transportation in the future as it does today.
    </p>

    <ArgonStableChart :height="200" class="mt-8 mb-10" />

    <p>
      In order to ensure each argon maintains a stable value, the number of argons in circulation continually rises and
      falls based on current supply and demand.
    </p>

    <h3>Argonots Are Your Operational Keys</h3>
    <p>
      Similar to Bitcoin, the Argonot has a fixed cap of 21 million tokens. There can never be more than 21M in
      circulation, and they are not designed to be used as a currency; instead, the Argonot is a governance-aligned
      token that represents the right to
      operate the core mining and vaulting services of the network. Without proof of Argonot ownership, you
      cannot actively mine or vault. Therefore the market value of Argonots should
      represent the market's perceived value of being an operator, which we expect to fluctuate over time.
    </p>
    <ArgonotVolatilityChart :height="200" class="mt-8 mb-10" />

    <h3>Bitcoins Become the Network's Underlying Store of Value</h3>
    <p>
      Although the Bitcoin Network is a separate network from Argon, Bitcoin tokens are a first-class citizen of
      the Argon Network. You can read more about this on our
      <router-link to="/docs/assets-and-entities/bitcoin-locks">Liquid Locking for Bitcoin</router-link> page. The
      important thing to understand is that although Bitcoin is tightly integrated into Argon, it's also exogenous to
      Argon, and therefore Bitcoin's price can fluctuate wildly with little impact on Argon.
    </p>
    <BitcoinVolatilityChart :height="200" class="mt-8 mb-10" />

    <h2>Mining</h2>
    <p>
      Mining is not always the most profitable action in the Argon Network, but it's the first step to understanding how
      Argon's economic model works.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let's start with how you become a miner.
    </p>

    <p>
      A set number of mining seats are auctioned by the network each day. Anyone who is an
      <router-link to="/docs/desktop-app/operations-extension">Operator</router-link> can submit a bid. The highest bidder wins.
      Winners earn the right to mine for a fixed term of ten days (or frames as we call them in the Argon world).
    </p>
    <p>
      Operators can win as many mining seats per day as they wish.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each bid must contain two things: a preset number of Argonots (this amount is the same for everyone) and a
      variable number of Argons, which ultimately determines who wins the auction.
    </p>
    <p>
      The following calculations panel is a visual representation of a mining bid and the possible returns that might result. The
      yellow shows which variables you can adjust. The three numbered items highlight the main factors that determine
      your profits (see below for more details).
    </p>

    <div class="border border-slate-900 rounded-lg px-2 mt-5 mb-10 bg-gray-900/90 text-white!">
      <MiningCalculator @updated="miningCalculatorUpdated" />
    </div>

    <p>
      The above return percentages are over a ten-day period, which can be difficult to understand. Most financial
      products use Annual Percentage Yield (APY), which shows your return over a year timeframe with the profits reinvested.
    </p>
    <p>
      Average Berkshire Hathaway Return Over Last Six Decades = {{ averageBerkshireHathawayAPY }}% APY
    </p>
    <p>
      Expected Mining Return of {{ numeral(expectedMiningTDR).format('0,0.[00]') }}% Over Ten Days = {{ numeral(expectedMiningAPY).format('0,0.[00]') }}% APY
    </p>
    <p>
      Actual Mining Return of {{ numeral(actualMiningTDR).format('0,0.[00]') }}% Over Ten Days = {{ numeral(actualMiningAPY).format('0,0.[00]') }}% APY
    </p>

    <p>
      The mining returns shown above are calculated on the network having 100 mining seats, which is the minimum number
      of mining seats possible. As the price-per-bid grows, the network adds additional seats similar to a stock split.
      This means the percentage returns shown above are accurate regardless of the number of seats. The max number of
      seats is 1,440.
    </p>

    <p>
      Also, the mining returns are driven by three main factors:
    </p>

    <h3>1. Your Bid Price</h3>
    <p>
      The Argonots in your bid are returned at the end of the mining cycle (ten days), however, the Argons are not
      returned. They are the cost of your bid. The Base Mining is a guaranteed quantity of tokens that is allocated to
      your mining seat (give or take a block), which allows you to bid knowing your base return. However, the tokens
      you receive can go much higher...
    </p>

    <h3>2. Argon Circulation Demand</h3>
    <p>
      Whenever the market demand drives the price of Argons above their target, the stabilization algorithm begins
      minting new argons. The longer and higher Argon's price stays above target, the more argons are minted. These newly
      minted argons are distributed to the miners through new blocks. By increasing the circulation, more argons will
      find their into the markets and help bring the price back down to target. This is pure profit for the miners.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Argonot Pricing Demand</h3>
    <p>
      The market price of Argonots can go up or down during your ten-day mining cycle. This can change your return you
      expected when you submitted your bid.
    </p>

    <h3>Following the Money</h3>
    <p>
      Argonots included in a bid are returned to the bidder at the end of the ten-day cycle,
      but the Argons are not. Argons paid into the auctions are distributed as follows:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-8">
      <li>
        <router-link to="/docs/assets-and-entities/argonot-stakes">10% is paid to the Argonot Stakes</router-link>
        used to secure these bids;
      </li>
      <li>
        <router-link to="/docs/assets-and-entities/upstream-nodes">20% is paid to the Upstream Nodes for onboarding</router-link>;
      </li>
      <li>
        <router-link to="/docs/assets-and-entities/vaulting-operations">the remainder is distributed to the vaults</router-link>
        and <router-link to="/docs/assets-and-entities/argon-bonds">Argon Bonds</router-link>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>The way these distributions are allocated create fascinating dynamic as shown below.</p>

    <h2>Argonot Staking</h2>
    <p>
      Stakes earn 10% of each day's mining auction revenue. There is no slashing of Stakes, and miners have
      no ability to divert the argonots staked to them. This gives Argonot Stakes a substantial upside in the revenue of the
      network while adding zero additional risk.
    </p>
    <p>
      Using the per-seat bid price from the Mining Calculator
      above, the total amount distributed to Argonot Stakes every ten days is as follows:
    </p>

    <div class="border border-gray-400 rounded-lg p-4 my-10">
      (₳{{ numeral(argonsBid).format('0,0.[00]') }} x {{ numeral(seatCount).format('0,0') }} seats
      = ₳{{ numeral(argonsBid * seatCount).format('0,0.[00]') }}) x 10% = ₳{{ numeral(payoutToArgonotStakesEveryTen).format('0,0.[00]') }}
    </div>

    <p>
      Only 40% of the Argonots in circulation can be staked. There are currently
      {{ micronotToArgonotNm(data.micronotsInCirculation).format('0,0') }} Argonots in circulation, which
      means the total number of allowed Stakes is:
    </p>
    <div class="border border-gray-400 rounded-lg p-4 my-10">
      ({{ micronotToArgonotNm(data.micronotsInCirculation).format('0,0') }} x 40%)
      = {{ numeral(maxArgonotStakes).format('0,0') }} Max Stakes
    </div>

    Which means each Stake is earning {{ numeral(payoutPerArgonotStakePerDay).format('0,0.[0000]') }} ARGN per day. If ARGNOT
    is priced on the market at ₳{{ numeral(argonForArgonot).format('0,0.00') }}, then your annual return would be:
    <div class="border border-gray-400 rounded-lg p-4 my-10 font-['Latin_Modern_Math']">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-2 text-2xl text-slate-900">
        <span class="font-medium">{{ numeral(argonotStakeAPY).format('0,0.[00]') }}% APY =</span>

        <span class="flex items-center">
          <span>(1 +&nbsp;</span>

          <!-- Fraction -->
          <span class="inline-flex flex-col items-center align-middle text-lg leading-none">
            <span class="border-b border-slate-900 px-1 pb-1">₳{{ numeral(payoutPerArgonotStakePerDay).format('0,0.[0000]') }}</span>
            <span class="px-1 pt-1">₳{{ numeral(data.usdForArgonot / data.usdForArgon).format('0,0.00') }}</span>
          </span>

          <span>)</span>

          <!-- Exponent -->
          <sup class="-ml-0.5 text-sm">365</sup>

          <span>&nbsp;− 1</span>
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      Because of the demand for Argonots within the network and in liquidity pools outside the network, only
      {{ numeral((data.activeArgonotStakes / maxArgonotStakes)*100).format('0,0') }}% of allowed staking volume is actually
      being used ({{ numeral(data.activeArgonotStakes).format('0,0') }}). This drives the actual returns of Argonot
      Stakes to {{ numeral(activeArgonotStakeAPY).format('0,0.[00]') }}% APY:
    </p>

    <div class="border border-gray-400 rounded-lg p-4 my-10 font-['Latin_Modern_Math']">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-2 text-2xl text-slate-900">
        <span class="font-medium">{{ numeral(activeArgonotStakeAPY).format('0,0.[00]') }}% APY =</span>

        <span class="flex items-center">
          <span>(1 +&nbsp;</span>

          <!-- Fraction -->
          <span class="inline-flex flex-col items-center align-middle text-lg leading-none">
            <span class="border-b border-slate-900 px-1 pb-1">₳{{ numeral(activePayoutPerArgonotStakePerDay).format('0,0.[0000]') }}</span>
            <span class="px-1 pt-1">₳{{ numeral(data.usdForArgonot / data.usdForArgon).format('0,0.00') }}</span>
          </span>

          <span>)</span>

          <!-- Exponent -->
          <sup class="-ml-0.5 text-sm">365</sup>

          <span>&nbsp;− 1</span>
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Computing the Yield-Supported Value of an Argonot</h3>
    <p>
      Argonot Stakes allow us to compare Argonots with other productive assets. Berkshire Hathaway, for example, has
      compounded at approximately {{ averageBerkshireHathawayAPY }}% annually over the last six decades.
    </p>

    <p>
      If today’s staking distribution remained fixed, an Argonot earning approximately ₳0.0044 per day would only need
      to rise from ₳6.02 to approximately ₳8.85 before its yield fell to 19.9%.
    </p>
    <p>
      But the staking distribution is not fixed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Argonots are included in the immutable rewards earned by miners. When Argonots become more valuable, the total
      value produced by a mining seat increases. Miners can therefore bid more Argons for that seat while preserving the
      same expected mining return.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because 10% of mining-seat auction revenue is distributed to Argonot Stakes, those larger bids produce larger
      staking distributions. A higher Argonot price can therefore increase both the value of the asset and the income it
      generates.
    </p>
    <p>
      Consider an illustrative price of ₳1,000 per Argonot. Under the current model, that price would support
      approximately:
    </p>

    <ul class="list-disc pl-8">
      <li>₳462,596 bid per mining seat</li>
      <li>₳0.6181 distributed to each Stake per day</li>
      <li>25.30% compounded annual staking yield</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="my-10 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-gray-400 p-4 font-['Latin_Modern_Math']">
      <table class="mx-auto border-separate border-spacing-x-3 border-spacing-y-2 text-lg sm:text-xl">
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="whitespace-nowrap text-right">₳6.02</td>
            <td>→</td>
            <td class="whitespace-nowrap text-right">₳0.00435 per day</td>
            <td>→</td>
            <td class="whitespace-nowrap text-right font-medium">30.18% APY</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="whitespace-nowrap text-right">₳1,000</td>
            <td>→</td>
            <td class="whitespace-nowrap text-right">₳0.6181 per day</td>
            <td>→</td>
            <td class="whitespace-nowrap text-right font-medium">25.30% APY</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      This means that increasing the Argonot price from approximately ₳6 to ₳1,000—a roughly 166-fold increase—does not
      reduce the modeled staking yield to something close to zero. The higher value of the Argonots mined through each
      seat supports larger auction bids, which replenish much of the yield distributed to Stakes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Under the model’s present assumptions, the staking yield approaches approximately 25.27% as the Argonot price
      rises. It therefore remains above Berkshire Hathaway’s historical 19.9% compound return even at an illustrative
      price of ₳1,000.
    </p>
    <p>
      This calculation does not establish that an Argonot must trade at ₳1,000. It demonstrates a narrower point: a
      ₳1,000 valuation is compatible with the current mining and staking mechanics and cannot be rejected merely because
      a higher purchase price would ordinarily compress yield.
    </p>
    <p>
      The calculation also assigns no additional value to the Argonot’s fixed supply, governance alignment, operational
      utility, or potential growth in demand for Argons and mining seats.
    </p>

    <h2>Vaulting</h2>
    <p>
      Vaulting is the magic that fully brings Argon's stablecoin into reality. It's also one of the most consistent
      revenue streams of the network. If you want to get into the intricate details of how it works, check out
      Vaulting Operations, Argon Bonds, and Bitcoin Liquid Locking.
    </p>

    <p>
      Vaults earn revenue in two ways:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-8">
      <li>Fees for Liquid Locking Bitcoins</li>
      <li>Argon Bond Distributions</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Each vaults is allowed to set their own fees for Liquid Locking. Because the benefit to Bitcoin holders is so
      enticing &ndash; it provides full liquidity at the market value of their bitcoin, and it hedges any downside
      pricing risk &ndash; the revenue potential will be quite strong. However, the fees for Argon Bonds are much
      stronger. So much so that we anticipate most vaults will effectively drive their fees to zero. Here's why:
    </p>

    <p>The more bitcoin locked in a vault, the higher a vault's revenue from bonds.</p>

    <div class="border border-slate-900 rounded-lg px-2 mt-5 mb-10 min-h-200 bg-gray-900/90 text-white!">
      <VaultingCalculator :argonsBid="argonsBid" :seatCount="seatCount" />
    </div>

    <h2>Argon Bonds</h2>


    <h2>Bitcoin Liquid Locking</h2>

  </DocContent>
  <DocContent v-else>Loading...</DocContent>
</template>

<script setup lang="ts">
import * as Vue from 'vue';
import BigNumber from "bignumber.js";
import numeral, { micronotToArgonotNm } from '@/lib/numeral';
import DocHeader from "@/screens/docs/DocHeader.vue";
import DocContent from "@/screens/docs/DocContent.vue";
import ArgonIcon from '@/assets/logo.svg?component';
import ArgonotIcon from '@/assets/argonot.svg?component';
import BitcoinIcon from '@/assets/bitcoin.svg?component';
import ArgonStableChart from '@/components/ArgonStableChart.vue';
import ArgonotVolatilityChart from '@/components/ArgonotVolatilityChart.vue';
import BitcoinVolatilityChart from '@/components/BitcoinVolatilityChart.vue';
import MiningCalculator from "@/components/calculators/MiningCalculator.vue";
import VaultingCalculator from "@/components/calculators/VaultingCalculator.vue";
import Data, {NetworkName} from "@/lib/Data";
import { defaultBasicsRecord, type IBasicsRecord } from "@/interfaces/IBasicsRecord";
import { micronotToArgonot } from "@/lib/currencyUtils";

const chainName = Vue.ref<NetworkName>(NetworkName.mainnet);
const data = Vue.ref<IBasicsRecord>(defaultBasicsRecord);
const isLoaded = Vue.ref(false);

const averageBerkshireHathawayAPY = 19.9;

const expectedMiningTDR = Vue.ref(0);
const expectedMiningAPY = Vue.ref(0);

const actualMiningTDR = Vue.ref(0);
const actualMiningAPY = Vue.ref(0);

const argonsBid = Vue.ref(0);
const seatCount = Vue.ref(0);

const maxArgonotStakes = Vue.computed(() => micronotToArgonot(data.value.micronotsInCirculation) * 0.4);

const payoutToArgonotStakesEveryTen = Vue.computed(() => {
  return argonsBid.value * seatCount.value * 0.1;
});

const payoutPerArgonotStakePerDay = Vue.computed(() => {
  const bn = BigNumber(payoutToArgonotStakesEveryTen.value).dividedBy(maxArgonotStakes.value).dividedBy(10);
  return bn.toNumber();
});

const argonForArgonot = Vue.computed(() => {
  return data.value.usdForArgonot / data.value.usdForArgon;
});

const argonotStakeAPY = Vue.computed(() => {
  return ((1 + payoutPerArgonotStakePerDay.value / 6.02) ** 365 - 1) * 100
});

const activePayoutPerArgonotStakePerDay = Vue.computed(() => {
  const bn = BigNumber(payoutToArgonotStakesEveryTen.value).dividedBy(data.value.activeArgonotStakes).dividedBy(10);
  return bn.toNumber();
});

const activeArgonotStakeAPY = Vue.computed(() => {
  return ((1 + activePayoutPerArgonotStakePerDay.value / 6.02) ** 365 - 1) * 100
});

function miningCalculatorUpdated(results: { expectedMiningTDR: number, actualMiningTDR: number, argonsBid: number, seatCount: number }) {
  expectedMiningTDR.value = results.expectedMiningTDR;
  actualMiningTDR.value = results.actualMiningTDR;
  expectedMiningAPY.value = (Math.pow(1 + results.expectedMiningTDR / 100, 36.5) - 1) * 100;
  actualMiningAPY.value = (Math.pow(1 + results.actualMiningTDR / 100, 36.5) - 1) * 100;

  argonsBid.value = results.argonsBid;
  seatCount.value = results.seatCount;
}

async function loadData() {
  data.value = await Data.fetchBasics(chainName.value);
}

Vue.onMounted(async () => {
  await loadData();
  isLoaded.value = true;
});
</script>

<style scoped>
@import "../../../main.css";

</style>
